# Day 4 — RISC-V CPU Core Implementation

## Overview

Day 4 focused on taking the concepts learned with RISC-V and Makerchip and using them to build the basic structure of a RISC-V processor.

The work was carried out incrementally, with individual processor blocks being developed and connected to form a functional CPU core.

## RISC-V CPU Microarchitecture

The processor was developed by implementing the major stages required for instruction execution.

The main blocks explored during this session were:

* Program Counter (PC)
* Instruction Memory
* Instruction Fetch
* Instruction Decode
* Register File Read
* Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)
* Register File Write
* Branch Logic

These blocks work together to fetch an instruction, understand what operation is required, perform the operation, and update the processor state.

## 1. Program Counter

The Program Counter keeps track of the address of the instruction that needs to be executed.

For sequential instructions, the PC is updated to point to the next instruction.

```text
Current PC
    ↓
Fetch Instruction
    ↓
Execute
    ↓
Update PC
    ↓
Next Instruction
```

## 2. Instruction Fetch

The instruction fetch section obtains the instruction corresponding to the current program-counter value.

The fetched instruction is then passed to the decoding logic for further processing.

## 3. Instruction Decode

The instruction decoder determines what type of RISC-V instruction has been fetched.

The decoded information is used to determine the required operands and the operation that the processor must perform.

## 4. Register File

The register file provides the processor with the register values required by an instruction.

It also provides a mechanism for writing the result of an operation back into the appropriate destination register.

## 5. Arithmetic Logic Unit

The ALU performs the required arithmetic or logical operation.

Depending on the instruction, the ALU can perform operations such as addition, subtraction, comparison, and logical operations.

## 6. Branch Logic

Branch instructions require the processor to determine whether a change in program flow is necessary.

The branch logic therefore works together with the PC to select the appropriate next instruction.

## Processor Flow

The overall execution flow can be represented as:

```text
       Program Counter
              ↓
      Instruction Fetch
              ↓
      Instruction Decode
              ↓
        Register Read
              ↓
             ALU
              ↓
       Register Write
              ↓
        Branch / Next PC
```

## Makerchip Implementation

The processor blocks were implemented and tested using the Makerchip environment and TL-Verilog.

The design was developed progressively so that individual parts could be observed and verified before combining them into the larger processor structure.

## Verification

Simulation and visualization were used to observe the behaviour of the processor.

The waveforms and visualizations helped in checking whether the instructions were progressing through the processor as expected.

## Learning Outcomes

By completing Day 4, I gained practical understanding of:

* Basic RISC-V CPU microarchitecture.
* Program-counter operation.
* Instruction fetching and decoding.
* Register-file read and write operations.
* ALU-based instruction execution.
* Branch handling.
* Connecting individual hardware blocks into a processor.
* Using Makerchip and TL-Verilog for processor implementation.

## Key Takeaway

Day 4 was the transition from learning individual RISC-V and TL-Verilog concepts to actually putting those concepts together to form a RISC-V CPU core.

The incremental implementation approach made it easier to understand how each hardware block contributes to the complete processor.

